course: dictionary
lesson: 14603
title: Poetic words and poem-like writing
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: «Poetic» comes from «poet», the person who writes poems. So when something is poetic, it is to do with poems, or it is written in a way that sounds like a poem.
explain: «The sunset over the sea was so poetic that Mia stopped to stare.» The clues are «so» and «stopped to stare». Mia did not stop because the sunset was ordinary. Poetic here means beautiful and full of feeling, like a poem.
explain: Poetic can also describe fair punishments. «Dan dropped litter all week, so he had to pick up litter in the park every day.» The punishment fits the wrong, so this is poetic justice. Always check your meaning by putting it back into the sentence: does it still make sense?
fact: «Poetic» is an adjective, and its more poetic and most poetic forms are used to compare things.
fact: «Poetic» means to do with poems, or written like a poem.
fact: «Poetic» can mean beautiful, expressive or imaginative, like a poem.
fact: Poetic justice is when someone who has done wrong gets a fitting, almost funny punishment.
fact: Poetic devices are tools poets use, such as rhyme, rhythm, alliteration, simile and metaphor.
exercise: «The sunset over the sea was so poetic that Mia stopped to stare.» What does poetic mean here? | Very noisy and loud | Written in a school textbook | Cold and wet from the rain | *Beautiful and full of feeling, like a poem
exercise: What is poetic justice? | A prize given for the best drawing | A long holiday for doing well | *A fitting punishment that matches the wrong, often in a neat or funny way | A poem that is read out loud at school
exercise: «Poetic» comes from which word? | potato | pretend | *poet | pocket
test: «Dan dropped litter all week, so he had to pick up litter in the park every day.» What is this an example of? | *Poetic justice | Rhythm | Alliteration | A simile
test: Which description is the most poetic? | The shop sold bread and milk | The door was red | The bus was late again | *The moon hung over the quiet lake like a silver lamp
exam: Which poetic device repeats the same starting sound, as in «silly snakes slid silently»? | *Alliteration | Rhythm | Metaphor | Rhyme
